module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Smooth.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Cotangent
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.ResidueField.Fiber
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Kaehler.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Finrank
import Sheaf.RingTheory.PresentationLocalizedConormalSplit
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Kaehler.TensorProduct
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Prod

/-! # Differential rank at points of fibres of a smooth polynomial quotient
Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, proofs of Lemmas 07CG and 07CH.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-smooth-quotient-fibre-differential-rank)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, proof of Lemma 07CH (the split
conormal sequence), applied to the fibres in the proof of Lemma 07CG.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-smooth-quotient-fibre-differential-rank)

Proof. Write Q for the smooth quotient and J for the ideal generated by the equations.
Present Q by its polynomial generators and the equations f. The kernel is J, so its
extension cotangent module identifies with J/J², compatibly with the Q-action. Apply
Algebra.Presentation.localized_conormal_equiv at a = 1. Transport along Q_1 ≃ Q,
using that localization at one changes neither the cotangent module nor differentials,
to obtain (J/J²) × Ω[Q/R] ≃ Q^n. This is the split conormal sequence of 07CH;
the given basis identifies its first summand with Q^c. Let p be a prime of R,
T = κ(p) ⊗[R] Q and q a prime of T, with residue field K. Tensor the split sequence
with K over Q; it remains split exact without any flatness assumption on K.
K ⊗[Q] (J/J²) has basis of cardinality c and K ⊗[Q] Q^n has basis of cardinality n.
Differential base change and tensor associativity identify the last summand with
K ⊗[T] Ω[T/κ(p)]. The dimension formula for this direct sum gives
c + dim_K(K ⊗[T] Ω[T/κ(p)]) = n, so c ≤ n and the dimension is n-c.
All spaces are finite-dimensional: the middle space is finite-dimensional, and
both summands inherit this property. The presence of q makes K a field even when
other fibres or Q are zero; if the fibre is zero there is no q, so no claim is made.
-/
theorem smooth_quotient_fibre_differential_rank
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {n c : ℕ} (f : Fin c → MvPolynomial (Fin n) R)
    [Smooth R (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f))]
    (b : Module.Basis (Fin c)
      (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f))
      (Ideal.span (Set.range f)).Cotangent)
    (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime]
    (q : PrimeSpectrum (p.Fiber (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f)))) :
    c ≤ n ∧ Module.finrank q.asIdeal.ResidueField
      (q.asIdeal.ResidueField ⊗[p.Fiber
        (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f))]
          KaehlerDifferential p.ResidueField
            (p.Fiber (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f)))) = n - c := by
  sorry

end Algebra
